intro:
In celestial mechanics, the specific relative angular momentum (often denoted 






              h
              →





    {\displaystyle {\vec {h}}}

 or 




          h



    {\displaystyle \mathbf {h} }

) of a body is the angular momentum of that body divided by its mass. In the case of two orbiting bodies it is the vector product of their relative position and relative linear momentum, divided by the mass of the body in question.
